ged Joe gang grants awards up  to five hundred dollars to employees who  have an idea on how to make work more  fun from producing the hot cocoa machine  for the company's freezer crew to a  stereo for the production crew Cohen and  Greenfield were willing to fund any  creative ideas that might make for  happier workers along with other  incentives and benefits such as allowing  workers bring their dogs to work or  jeans if the offers workers chose to  according the corn in Greenfield we  thought why don't we get together and do  something fun and be our own bosses and  since we like to eat we should do  something with food we wanted our  workers to work hard but do it in an  environment that was fun  no one wants to dread going to work our  employees are what makes Ben and Jerry's  successful action item number three  challenge the big guys whatever industry  you're in  they're probably big companies already  dominating your market don't be afraid  of them just find a different way to do  things smaller companies are more agile  chemic decisions quickly can provide  better service and can get closer to the  customers use those benefits as a way to  challenge the big guys and start winning  some market share corn and green filled  with just a couple of hippies try to  avoid becoming simply another big  company living through the 1960s a to  dislike business for all of its negative  social and environmental effects 1984  big business came out to the little guys  pillsbury the multi-million dollar  company behind haagen-dazs began to feel  threatened by the rapid growth of ben  and jerry's an attempt to shut down the  young upstarts Pillsbury game Ben and  Jerry's distributors throughout Boston  an ultimatum Sal haagen-dazs or Sal Ben  and Jerry's but not both corn and  Greenfield were not about to let this  corporate giant shut them down after  finding little hope and their legal  options the two decided to take matters  into their own hands together they  launched t